(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-metabolism-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Quiz 2 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"The liver protects the body from many ingested and internally produced chemicals by...","choices":["converting toxins into oxygen for the tissues to use","metabolizing drugs and toxins into forms that can be excreted","secreting toxins unchanged directly into the bloodstream","storing all toxins permanently within its own cells in this scenario"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hepatocytes carry out biotransformation, chemically modifying drugs, hormones, and toxins (often making them more water-soluble) so they can be excreted in bile or urine; this detoxifying role is a central hepatic function. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 8-10"},{"q":"Failure of the liver to clear ammonia is dangerous because ammonia...","choices":["is the main metabolic fuel used by the beating cardiac muscle","is required in large amounts for red cell production","is harmless and simply excreted unchanged by the lungs","is toxic to the nervous system and can impair brain function"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ammonia produced by amino-acid deamination and gut bacteria is neurotoxic; the liver normally converts it to urea for excretion. When hepatic function fails, ammonia accumulates and can cause hepatic encephalopathy — confusion, altered consciousness, and impaired brain function. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 10; Ch 70, slide 16"},{"q":"The liver serves as a major storage site for...","choices":["oxygen bound to hemoglobin for release during exercise","digestive enzymes awaiting release into the duodenum","glycogen, iron, and several fat-soluble vitamins","mature lymphocytes ready to fight infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The liver stores glycogen (a glucose reserve), iron (as ferritin), and fat-soluble vitamins such as A and D, acting as a central metabolic warehouse the body can draw on between meals. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 2, 8"},{"q":"A hallmark synthetic function of the liver is production of...","choices":["plasma proteins such as albumin and most clotting factors","digestive amylase secreted into the small intestine","erythropoietin that stimulates red cell production","insulin and glucagon that closely regulate blood glucose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The liver synthesizes the majority of plasma proteins, including albumin (the main determinant of colloid osmotic pressure) and most coagulation factors, which is why liver disease produces both edema and bleeding tendencies. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 2, 11"},{"q":"The brain relies heavily on a steady glucose supply because it...","choices":["normally uses glucose as its main fuel and stores very little of its own","stores large amounts of glycogen for its own use during this process in the tissues","produces its own glucose independently of the blood","can only use fatty acids delivered from adipose tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Under normal conditions the brain depends on a continuous supply of blood glucose as its primary fuel and holds almost no energy reserve of its own, which is why hypoglycemia rapidly impairs brain function (though ketones can partly substitute during prolonged fasting). — Ch 68, slides 25-27; Ch 73, slide 12"},{"q":"Epinephrine and glucagon raise blood glucose partly through phosphorylation, which...","choices":["inactivates phosphorylase so stored glycogen is protected from breakdown","activates glycogen phosphorylase while inactivating glycogen synthase","activates both glycogen phosphorylase and glycogen synthase together","activates glycogen synthase to build glycogen from incoming glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: phosphorylation activates phosphorylase (breakdown) and inactivates synthase (storage), favoring glycogenolysis. — Ch 68, slides 11-12"},{"q":"Phosphorylating glucose to glucose-6-phosphate as it enters a cell serves to...","choices":["trap glucose inside the cell by preventing it from diffusing back out","release energy that the cell immediately uses for contraction","signal the pancreas to stop secreting insulin into the bloodstream right away","convert glucose into fat for long-term storage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adding a charged phosphate group keeps glucose from crossing the membrane back out, effectively trapping it inside so it is committed to intracellular metabolism (glycolysis, glycogen synthesis, or the pentose pathway). — Ch 68, slides 6-7"},{"q":"Between meals, the liver keeps blood glucose from falling by...","choices":["absorbing glucose taken from resting muscle in order to store it centrally","converting glucose into glycogen for storage","breaking down stored glycogen and releasing glucose (glycogenolysis)","halting all glucose release to conserve it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the postabsorptive state the liver depolymerizes its glycogen (glycogenolysis) and, as that store runs low, makes new glucose (gluconeogenesis), releasing glucose into the blood to prevent hypoglycemia. This is its glucose-buffer function. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 9"},{"q":"Chylomicrons and VLDL both carry triglyceride, but they differ in that...","choices":["chylomicrons are made in the liver, while VLDL comes from the intestine under these conditions","both particles are made only within adipose tissue purely for local fat storage","chylomicrons carry cholesterol back to the liver, while VLDL carries dietary fat","chylomicrons carry dietary fat from the gut, while VLDL carries fat made by the liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chylomicrons are assembled in the intestine to transport exogenous (dietary) fat, whereas VLDL is made by the liver to export endogenous triglyceride (much of it synthesized from carbohydrate); both deliver fatty acids to tissues via lipoprotein lipase. — Ch 69, slide 6; GI Physiology, slide 139"},{"q":"Very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) is produced by the liver mainly to...","choices":["deliver dissolved oxygen to the working tissues during periods of heavy exercise","export triglycerides, largely made from carbohydrate, to peripheral tissues","return excess cholesterol from tissues back to the liver","carry dietary fat absorbed directly from the intestine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The liver packages triglycerides (much of it synthesized from excess carbohydrate) into VLDL and exports them to peripheral tissues, where lipoprotein lipase releases fatty acids for use or storage. — Ch 69, slide 6"},{"q":"During prolonged fasting, the liver produces ketone bodies because...","choices":["high fatty-acid oxidation generates acetyl-CoA in excess of TCA cycle capacity","rising insulin stimulates the liver to store its excess fat as ketone bodies","abundant circulating glucose drives the conversion of pyruvate directly into ketones","amino acids are unavailable, forcing protein to be turned into ketones"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: heavy beta-oxidation floods the liver with acetyl-CoA beyond what the citric acid cycle can handle, so it is diverted to ketones. — Ch 69, slides 15-19"},{"q":"Cortisol influences protein metabolism by...","choices":["stimulating net protein synthesis in all tissues","preventing any amino acids at all from entering the blood during stress or fasting states","blocking the liver from making any new glucose during stress or fasting states","promoting breakdown of tissue protein to supply amino acids for gluconeogenesis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol (a glucocorticoid) drives catabolism of extrahepatic tissue protein, raising plasma amino acids that the liver uses for gluconeogenesis; this helps maintain blood glucose during stress and fasting. — Ch 70, slide 19; Ch 68, slide 30"},{"q":"Before an amino acid's carbon skeleton can be used for energy, its amino group is removed by...","choices":["the electron transport chain during ATP synthesis under these conditions","deamination or transamination, freeing the skeleton for metabolism","glycolysis, which strips the nitrogen as it makes ATP","beta-oxidation, which removes nitrogen two atoms at a time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Removing the amino group by deamination (releasing ammonia) or transamination (transferring it to a keto acid) frees the carbon skeleton to enter energy pathways as a glucogenic or ketogenic intermediate, while the nitrogen is routed to urea. — Ch 70, slides 14-17"},{"q":"Amino acids that are degraded to acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA are termed ketogenic because they...","choices":["can be converted into glucose through gluconeogenesis","release ammonia that is directly excreted by the lungs","give rise to ketone bodies rather than to new glucose","are used only to build new proteins, never for fuel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ketogenic amino acids feed into acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA, which can form ketone bodies but cannot be used to make net glucose (since acetyl-CoA cannot be converted back to pyruvate for gluconeogenesis). — Ch 70, slide 18"},{"q":"Which statement about amino-acid metabolism is correct?","choices":["Transamination permanently destroys the carbon skeleton of the amino acid","Transamination builds nonessential amino acids; deamination frees ammonia for urea","Both reactions occur only in muscle tissue and never take place within the liver","Deamination adds an amino group onto a carbon skeleton, a step needing vitamin B6"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: transamination (needing vitamin B6) forms nonessential amino acids, while deamination liberates ammonia that becomes urea. — Ch 70, slides 7-16"},{"q":"Leptin and ghrelin have opposing effects on appetite because...","choices":["leptin drives hunger during fasting, while ghrelin signals satiety after meals","both are released from adipose tissue but act on different brain regions","leptin from the stomach stimulates appetite, while ghrelin suppresses it","leptin from fat reduces appetite, while ghrelin from the stomach stimulates it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: leptin from adipose tissue signals satiety and reduces intake, while ghrelin from the stomach stimulates hunger. — Ch 72, slides 11-14"},{"q":"During early starvation, before fat stores are heavily used, the body first relies on...","choices":["stored glycogen, which is exhausted within roughly a day","ketone bodies, which are the very first fuel mobilized","body protein, which is broken down before any glycogen","dietary intake, which continues to supply energy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The body first draws on liver (and muscle) glycogen for glucose, but these stores are small and are largely depleted within about 24 hours; the body then shifts increasingly to fat oxidation and, later, ketone production and protein breakdown. — Ch 72, slides 17-19"},{"q":"Ghrelin, secreted largely by the stomach, influences feeding by...","choices":["digesting fats directly within the small intestine","suppressing hunger over the long term as fat stores grow","stimulating hunger and rising before meals to prompt eating","ending each meal by signaling fullness to the brain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ghrelin is often called the 'hunger hormone': its levels rise before meals and fall after eating, and it acts on the hypothalamus to stimulate appetite, in contrast to satiety signals like leptin and CCK that reduce intake. — Ch 72, slides 11-14"},{"q":"Per gram, the energy yields of the macronutrients are approximately...","choices":["9 kcal for carbohydrate, 9 for protein, and 4 for fat","4 kcal for carbohydrate, 4 for protein, and 9 for fat","4 kcal for fat, with carbohydrate and protein at 9 kcal","equal energy from all three, at about 4 kcal per gram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbohydrate and protein each provide about 4 kcal per gram, while fat provides about 9 kcal per gram; fat's much higher energy density is why it is the body's most compact form of energy storage. — Ch 72, slides 4-5"},{"q":"Gram for gram, fat provides more than twice the energy of carbohydrate or protein because it...","choices":["requires no oxygen to release its stored chemical energy","is absorbed far more completely from the gut than either carbohydrate or protein","yields about 9 kcal/g versus roughly 4 kcal/g for carbohydrate and protein","is stored together with large amounts of water that add to its measured energy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: fat provides ~9 kcal/g, over double the ~4 kcal/g of carbohydrate or protein. — Ch 72, slide 4"},{"q":"Thyroid hormone influences metabolic rate by...","choices":["lowering metabolic rate to conserve energy during fasting","raising the resting metabolic rate of most body tissues","acting only on skeletal muscle during exercise","having no measurable effect on basal metabolism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thyroid hormone increases the metabolic activity of nearly all tissues, raising basal metabolic rate and heat production; this is why thyroid status is a major determinant of BMR. — Ch 73, slide 9"},{"q":"Basal metabolic rate represents...","choices":["the minimal energy needed to sustain the body while at rest and fasting","the energy used only for digesting and absorbing a meal","total daily energy output including all physical activity","the peak energy expenditure that is achievable during heavy physical exercise"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: basal metabolic rate is the minimal energy to maintain the body at rest, measured fasting and thermoneutral. — Ch 73, slides 8, 10"},{"q":"The thermic effect of food refers to...","choices":["the heat lost through sweating during a meal","the surplus calories that are stored away as fat after overeating","the energy used only during physical activity","the extra energy expended to digest, absorb, and process a meal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The thermic effect of food is the rise in energy expenditure required to digest, absorb, and metabolize nutrients; it is modest for carbohydrate and fat but substantial for protein (which can raise metabolic rate up to about 30%). — Ch 73, slide 10"},{"q":"A respiratory quotient near 1.0 shortly after a carbohydrate meal reflects that...","choices":["protein breakdown dominates the fuel mix, fixing the ratio at exactly 0.8","carbohydrate oxidation produces CO2 and consumes O2 in roughly equal amounts","fat is the main fuel, releasing little CO2 relative to the O2 used","no gas exchange is occurring at all because metabolism has temporarily paused"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: burning carbohydrate gives an RQ near 1.0 because CO2 produced and O2 consumed are about equal. — Ch 72, slides 5-6"},{"q":"When a fever finally 'breaks,' the person often flushes and sweats because the hypothalamic set point has...","choices":["returned to normal, so the now-high body temperature is shed by sweating and vasodilation","risen further, causing the body to generate even more heat","been abolished, leaving temperature completely unregulated","dropped far below normal, triggering intense shivering during this particular process in the tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When pyrogen levels fall, the set point drops back to normal; the body temperature is now above the set point, so the hypothalamus activates heat-loss responses — vasodilation and sweating — to bring temperature down, producing the flush and sweat of the crisis. — Ch 74, slides 18-20"},{"q":"Sweating cools the body only when...","choices":["it is reabsorbed back into the sweat glands during this particular process","the surrounding air is already fully saturated with water","it remains as liquid droplets on the skin surface","the sweat actually evaporates, absorbing heat from the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cooling comes from the evaporation of sweat, which absorbs heat (the latent heat of vaporization) from the skin; sweat that merely drips off without evaporating removes little heat, which is why humid conditions blunt sweat's cooling effect. — Ch 74, slides 6-9"},{"q":"Shivering raises body temperature by...","choices":["producing heat through rapid, involuntary muscle contractions","increasing evaporative water loss from the airways and skin","lowering the hypothalamic set point toward the cold","shunting warm blood from the core to the skin surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Shivering is driven by the hypothalamic motor center for shivering; rapid involuntary muscle contractions generate substantial metabolic heat (potentially several-fold above resting), warming the body in the cold. — Ch 74, slide 15"},{"q":"To conserve heat in the cold, sympathetic signals cause skin blood vessels to...","choices":["increase sweating to trap a warm surface layer","leak plasma into tissues to insulate the body","constrict, reducing heat transfer from the core to the surface","dilate widely, bringing much more warm blood to the skin surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sympathetic activation produces cutaneous vasoconstriction, cutting blood flow to the skin so that less core heat is carried to the surface where it could be lost; the skin radiator is effectively turned down. — Ch 74, slides 11-14"},{"q":"The hypothalamus acts as the body's thermostat, with the responses to cold coordinated so that the body...","choices":["raises its set point to induce a protective fever","loses heat through vasodilation and sweating during this process in the tissues","conserves and generates heat through vasoconstriction and shivering","stops all metabolic heat production to save energy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When cold is sensed, the hypothalamus triggers heat-conserving and heat-generating responses: cutaneous vasoconstriction to reduce loss and shivering (plus increased metabolism) to produce heat, defending core temperature. — Ch 74, slides 4, 11-15"},{"q":"Sweat reaching the skin surface is normally hypotonic to plasma because...","choices":["potassium fully replaces sodium before the sweat reaches the surface","aldosterone actively adds extra salt into the sweat as it initially forms","the sweat glands secrete pure water that lacks any electrolytes","sodium and chloride are reabsorbed from the duct, especially at low flow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: as primary secretion moves through the duct, Na+ and Cl- are reabsorbed, leaving hypotonic sweat, most markedly at low flow rates. — Ch 74, slide 8"}]};
